F. 
Data Structures:

I used various different data structures in this project.

1. Struct: I firstly used a struct entitled Count in order to store the 
frequencry of each character. I wanted to store three pieces of data the letter
its frequency and its encoding all together in a singular place. 
Thus I used a struct with each  struct storing a char variable, int variable, 
and a string. This was the correct choice because it allowed me to tie this 
data together and have my code consider it as one item holding three things. 
I could use Structs whenever I need to hold multiple pieces of data in one 
holder. An example of a different situation when I could use it would be if I 
were coding a database system for a vet storing all their patients. Each struct
would represent a patient and hold a string for the name, an int for the age, 
another string for the type of animal, and another string storing the patient's
history. A second example could be a school database system. The struct could 
hold the student's name in a string, their ID number in an int, and their major
 in a string.  

2. Vector: In order to store all the Structs I created for every unique 
character, I used a vector. This is because I did not know how many unique
characters there were, so I needed a data structure that could change size, and
I wanted to be able to access any element easily. I also used that same vector
to store all the letters and their coressponding encodings. I first cleared it
and then loaded it with the Count struct storing each character and encoding.
Another example of when to use a vector would be a program that needs to store 
all the station objects of a metro system simulator and the amount of stations 
is not specified and the user needs easy access to each station. A second 
example would be an application that needs to store all exam scores in one 
place and there is an unknown amount of grades and needs to be easy access to 
each grade. 

3. Tree: Finally, I used a tree for both the encoding and decoidng processes. 
As part of the Huffman Coding process, it is necesary to build a tree with the
more frequent characters closer to the root, and the less frequent farther. It 
was the right choice for this project because it allows to decode faster 
because only a part of the data strucutre is needed to sift through to find the
value thus decoding process has a complexity of O(logn) and for the encoding 
process it made sense because it allowed to decrease the amount of bits needed
to encode some of the characters. Two other times a tree would be useful would 
be firstly if I needed to organize exam scores in ascending order and be able
to find the one I need quickly than I can use a BST and find it in O(logn) 
time. Secondly, an example would be when coding a family tree, then a tree
would should whose child is whose well. 

Algorithm Overview:

An interesting algorithm was the code that I wrote for the char_codes() 
function. Here I created the table of every letter and their corresponding 
encoding recursively. I thought this algorithm was cool because it really
showed off the benefit of using a Huffman Tree to encode data because you 
simply assign the node of the left to 0 and the node on the right to 1, and
you keep doing that until everything has a matching encoding. By the same 
reasoning, I also enjoined the opposite function in the decoder section, which
would decode bit string into a letter. I found it so simple to just go down the
tree based on the current digit (whether 0 or 1) as opposed to just coding 
every letter into itd 8-bit binary string. I also really liked the use of get
here as it allowed to me to just move down the stream no matter which function
or iteration of the recursion I was in. 

G. Testing:

phaseOne.cpp/phaseOne.h:
For phase one of the project I tested using unit_test. For each of the three
functions I created, I had multiple tests that would test the basic cases
and various edge cases. This allowed me to see very easily when my code 
wasn't working for a specific input because all the tests were independent of 
each other. For count_freqs() I first tested the function would work for a 
basic singular word input. Then I tested it against large input, an input with
only the same letter, and an empty inpit. Then for searilize and deserialize
I tested on the tree produced by ZapUtil. I first serialized it and made sure 
it corresponded to the version in the text and then deserialized it.

HuffmanCoder.cpp/HuffmanCoder.h:
For phase 2 of the project, I tested using diff testing. Firstly, I tested 
the encoder function. I used the provided banana.txt file as a simple file to 
provide to the code as it had all different letters. I compared the amount of
bits it compressed to, to the amount of bits the_zap compressed it to. Then I 
tested using sentences.txt, ecoli.coli, allA.txt, hi.txt, apple_banana. Next 
for stress tests I used all_conll_english.txt and works_of_shakespeare.txt. 
After all these files ran, I compared the amount of bits to the amount of bits 
that the demo compressed it to. Finally I made some on my own tests such as 
empty.file and singleLetter.txt to test more edge cases such as if the file was
empty or had only one letter. Lastly, I gave the code invalid commands like an 
input name that did not exist or more that 4 things in my run command to see if
my code gave the same error message as the demo.  

